<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khmer Rouge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1960s–<a href="/years/1999">1999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Communist Party of Kampuchea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> Rouge was the name given to the Communist Party of Kampuchea and its armed forces. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot">Pol Pot</a>, it fought the Khmer Republic, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> in April 1975 and ruled as Democratic Kampuchea, emptying the cities and causing the deaths of around 1.5 to 2 million people through execution, forced labour and famine. Overthrown by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in 1979, it fought on from the Thai border until its collapse in the late 1990s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge.jpg" alt="Khmer Rouge" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This (SVG on base of source) was created by me. See File History, below, for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Democratic_Kampuchea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Sino-Soviet split, open by the early 1960s, divided the communist world, and Mao’s doctrine of rural revolution inspired movements from India’s Naxalites to Peru’s Shining Path, while China backed communist forces in Vietnam and the <b>Khmer Rouge</b> in Cambodia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After repeated attacks by the <b>Khmer Rouge</b> regime of Democratic Kampuchea on border villages, Vietnamese forces invaded Cambodia on 25 December 1978, took Phnom Penh on 7 January 1979 and installed the People’s Republic of Kampuchea under Heng Samrin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…millions passed through the camps of the Gulag; the famine caused by the Great Leap Forward in China killed tens of millions between 1959 and 1961; and the <b>Khmer Rouge</b> regime in Cambodia caused the deaths of between 1.5 and 2 million people in less than four years.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Khmer Rouge</b> regime in Cambodia killed or starved to death between a fifth and a quarter of the population from 1975 to 1979.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Maoists, for their part, contend that the <b>Khmer Rouge</b> strongly deviated from Marxist doctrine.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Work stopped in the <b>Khmer Rouge</b> era: the Conservation was disbanded in 1975, Khmer Rouge forces burned the remaining wood as fuel, and a shoot-out with Vietnamese forces left bullet holes in some reliefs.</blockquote><a href="/work/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
